urns\r\n4. Diabetes mellitus\r\n5. Pressure and tissue ischemia\r\n6. Frostbite\r\n\r\nTypes of Gangrene\r\n\r\n1. Dry gangrene\r\n\r\n* Usually caused by progressive loss of arterial blood supply.\r\n* Tissue becomes dry, shriveled and black.\r\n* Usually develops slowly.\r\n* There is a clear line of demarcation between healthy and dead tissue.\r\n* Infection is initially minimal.\r\n\r\n2. Wet gangrene\r\n\r\n* Occurs when ischemic tissue becomes infected.\r\n* Tissue is swollen, soft and discolored.\r\n* Pus and foul-smelling discharge may be present.\r\n* Spreads rapidly.\r\n* Can lead to sepsi\n<truncated 2504 bytes>\n3. \"C:\\Users\\renu5\\Downloads\\Tier1_EasyWins_CheatSheet.md\" Do the same for this. \n4. I'm not able to understand it. Make a few changes to the format as I have given it to you, but in a compact way as you have done above. Mix it up so I can understand properly. \n5. c:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\cc5ae5f0-3c52-483a-b0d6-e323018f022c\\Missing_Theory_Answers.txt          do same for this\n6. Question: Explain various casting defects with causes and remedies.\r\n\r\nAnswer (Write 5-6 for 7M):\r\n\r\nBLOW HOLES Cause: Moisture in sand, low permeability, excessive gas generation. Remedy: Use dry sand. Improve venting. Increase permeability of moulding sand.\r\n\r\nMISRUN (Incomplete filling) Cause: Pouring temperature too low, thin sections in casting, improper gating. Remedy: Increase pouring temperature. Redesign gating system to ensure proper flow.\r\n\r\nSHRINKAGE CAVITY Cause: Insufficient molten metal to feed the casting during solidification. No riser or riser is too small. Remedy: Use adequately sized risers. Place risers at the last point to solidify (hot spot).\r\n\r\nHOT TEARS (Hot Cracks) Cause: The casting cannot shrink freely because the mould or core is too rigid (poor collapsibility). Remedy: Improve collapsibility of sand. Add proper fillets at corners. Avoid sharp changes in section thickness.\r\n\r\nCOLD SHUT Cause: Two streams of molten metal meet but do not fuse because they have cooled too much. Re\n<truncated 1628 bytes>\n7. Question: Explain the properties of moulding sand.\r\n\r\nAnswer (Write 6-7 properties for 7M, any 4 for 4M):\r\n\r\nPermeability: The ability of moulding sand to allow gases and steam to pass through it during pouring. If permeability is low, gases get trapped and cause blow holes in the casting.\r\n\r\nFlowability (Plasticity): The ability of sand to flow around the pattern and take the exact shape of the mould cavity. Better flowability = better reproduction of fine details.\r\n\r\nCollapsibility: The ability of the mould to collapse (break down) easily after the casting solidifies. If sand does not collapse, the casting cannot shrink freely and internal stresses or cracks develop.\r\n\r\nCohesiveness (Strength): The ability of sand grains to stick together. Without cohesiveness, the mould walls will crumble when molten metal is poured.\r\n\r\nRefractoriness: The ability of sand to withstand high temperatures of molten metal without fusing or melting. Silica sand has high refractoriness (~1700 deg C).\r\n\r\nGreen Strength: The st\n<truncated 1795 bytes>\n8. fist chck did i do it above these texgt or not then do it for this text ; Question: Discuss various types of rolling mill arrangements.\r\n\r\nAnswer (Draw neat sketch for each):\r\n\r\nTWO-HIGH MILL (Non-Reversing) Two rolls rotating in one fixed direction. Workpiece passes in one direction only. Must be returned to the entry side for next pass. Slow.\r\n\r\nTWO-HIGH REVERSING MILL Same as above but roll direction can be reversed. Work passes back and forth. Eliminates return time. Used for blooming/slabbing.\r\n\r\nTHREE-HIGH MILL Three rolls stacked vertically. Metal passes forward between bottom and middle rolls, then backward between middle and top rolls. No reversing needed. Faster than two-high.\r\n\r\nFOUR-HIGH MILL Two small-diameter work rolls (in contact with metal) backed by two